			<!-- Section 1: The Haversine Formula -->
			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
					1. The Haversine Geodesic Formula: Why Great-Circle Calculations Matter in the Philippines
				</h2>
				<p class="text-sm text-mapa-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					The Philippine archipelago spans across more than 1,850 kilometers from the northernmost tip of Mavulis Island, Batanes (21.2° N) down to Sitangkai, Tawi-Tawi (4.7° N). Because standard flat web maps (such as Web Mercator EPSG:3857) exaggerate linear distances as you move away from the equator, measuring distance as a straight planar line on a computer screen introduces significant distortions.
				</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-mapa-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					This tool computes true <strong>great-circle distance</strong> (the shortest path between two points on the surface of a spherical Earth) using the spherical law of haversines:
				</p>
				<div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas border border-mapa-contour/70 font-mono text-xs text-mapa-ink mb-4 overflow-x-auto shadow-xs">
					a = sin²(&Delta;&phi; / 2) + cos(&phi;₁) &middot; cos(&phi;₂) &middot; sin²(&Delta;&lambda; / 2)<br/>
					c = 2 &middot; atan2(&radic;a, &radic;(1 - a))<br/>
					d = R &middot; c &emsp;[where R = 6,371.0 km (Earth mean radius)]
				</div>
				<p class="text-xs text-mapa-clay leading-relaxed">
					For Philippine civil aviation and maritime navigation, calculations are referenced to the <strong>WGS84 ellipsoid</strong> (World Geodetic System 1984), ensuring exact synchronization with global GPS satellites and nautical radar systems.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 2: Nautical Miles vs. Statute Miles -->
			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
					2. Nautical Miles (NM) vs. Kilometers in Philippine Maritime Law
				</h2>
				<p class="text-sm text-mapa-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					Under regulations enforced by the <strong>Maritime Industry Authority (MARINA)</strong> and the <strong>Philippine Coast Guard (PCG)</strong>, all vessel cruising logs, passenger vessel safety certificates, and navigational charts are expressed in <strong>nautical miles (NM)</strong> and knots (nautical miles per hour):
				</p>
				<ul class="space-y-3 text-xs text-mapa-ink/80 list-disc pl-5 leading-relaxed">
					<li>
						<strong>1 International Nautical Mile = 1,852 meters (1.852 km):</strong> Exactly 1 minute (1/60th of a degree) of latitude along any meridian of longitude. This makes it effortless for sailors and navigators to measure distances directly off latitude tick marks on physical nautical charts.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Knots (Speed):</strong> 1 knot equals 1 nautical mile per hour (1.852 km/h). Philippine fastcraft catamarans (such as OceanJet, SuperCat, and FastCat) cruise between 24 to 30 knots, whereas large cargo-passenger RoRo liners (such as 2GO Group and Trans-Asia Shipping) operate economically between 13 to 17 knots.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Statute Miles (1,609.34 meters):</strong> The traditional land mile, rarely used in Philippine aviation or maritime, but commonly encountered in American-manufactured aviation operating handbooks.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

